breaking_changes.Adaptation_method:
Update invocations of affected async methods to use 'await' inside async functions or .then() with Promises if omitting the final callback. 

Example:
// Before (callback required)
async.mapLimit(urls, 5, fn, callback);
// After (callback omitted, now returns Promise):
await async.mapLimit(urls, 5, fn);

If you wish to continue using callbacks, pass them as before; the methods will continue to support callback style. For utility methods (e.g., async.timeout, async.memoize), awaitable support is not introduced by this change—retain existing usage for those methods.

Change event-style property assignments to method invocations.
Before:
q.drain = function() { ... }
q.saturated = function() { ... }
q.empty = fn;
q.error = fn;
q.unsaturated = fn;
After:
q.drain(function() { ... })
q.saturated(function() { ... })
q.empty(fn)
q.error(fn)
q.unsaturated(fn)
To await the event:
await q.drain()
await q.empty()

If your logic uses 'callback(false)' (or passes 'false' as the first argument to a step callback) in any of the affected async control flow methods, thoroughly review how your application manages cancellation, completion, and error handling. Ensure you do not depend on the final callback being called after 'callback(false)' is used. If further cleanup or finalization is required, refactor your code to trigger it manually when canceling:

Example (was):
async.waterfall([
  function(cb) { ... cb(); },
  function(cb) { ... cb(false); }, // breaks chain, used to call 'final callback', now it won't
  function(cb) { ... cb(); }
], function(err) { /* final callback, used for cleanup, now never called if canceled */ })

Adaptation (now):
function step1(cb) { ... cb(); }
function step2(cb) { ... /* cancel */ cb(false); performCleanup(); }
function step3(cb) { ... cb(); }
function finalCallback(err) { /* finalization code */ }

async.waterfall([
  step1,
  step2,
  step3
], finalCallback);

function performCleanup() {
  // Call cleanup logic here because finalCallback will not be invoked after cancellation
}

Additionally, review code for potential memory leaks or resource retention that depended on final callback execution. Audit all uses of the listed async methods for unintended breakage.

If you need to cache error results for specific use cases, you must implement custom caching. For example:

Before:
// Errors automatically cached
const memoizedFn = async.memoize(originalFn);
memoizedFn('arg', callback); // both error and result cached per arguments

After:
// Implement custom error caching if needed
const cache = new Map();
const memoizedFn = async.memoize((arg, cb) => {
  if (cache.has(arg)) {
    cb(...cache.get(arg));
    return;
  }
  originalFn(arg, (err, result) => {
    if (err) {
      cache.set(arg, [err, result]);
    }
    cb(err, result);
  });
});

Update all usage of async.applyEach and async.applyEachSeries to use the new returned function interface. Example:
Before:
async.applyEach(fns, arg1, arg2, cb); // Partial application with arguments
After:
const fn = async.applyEach(fns);
// To use with a callback:
fn(cb)
// To use with Promises/async:
await fn()
// Note: Passing arguments for partial application is no longer supported. Refactor code to pass all needed arguments via the original function array or closures, not via applyEach itself.
